 "N174": {"text":"Scheibe, R. R. (2002) An Overview of Studded and Studless Tire Traction and Safety, WA-RD 551.1, Washington State Transportation Center for WSDOT/FHWA, Table 4 (data of Nordstrom & Samuelsson, VTI): Studded tyres on smooth ice at -1 to -14 C: friction 0.14-0.26 (optimum slip), 0.12-0.19 (locked wheel); 0.18-0.27 on stud-roughened ice. Ichihara report not found","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N176": {"text":"Design: Mid's soldiers (02-rules-units.js UNIT, spear to treb; 04-rules-tech.js the barracks, range, stable, smith and siege lines and their tiers; 06-rules-tuning.js TEACH_TO; 08-core.js stats, armyOf, BUSY, armyFree, armyRoom, clsMax, armyFull; 01-rules-basics.js POPMAX, ARMYMAX, BNSIZE; 39-huddle.js kindOf; 18-figures.js KIT, person, ELITE_LOOK, elitePennant, sprite, elephant and the looks) by Albert's call, Nov 5 10:26: the Unit shell, each kind its settings (age, class, level, look, speed, health); health and damage open for his play test","opened":true,"kind":"design"},
 "N177": {"text":"Asian elephant: San Diego Zoo Wildlife Alliance factsheet; arrows/bolts/guns: Williams, 'What can scientific analysis tell us about the effectiveness of medieval arms and armour' (citing Bergman et al. 1988; Krenn 1990); sword: HEMA Penzance (2026): Male Asian elephant ~3,600 kg avg (to 6,000), 2.7 m shoulder; crossbow (90 lb) bolt 62 m/s, ~100 g; matchlock arquebus muzzle 449 m/s; arming sword 0.9-1.3 kg. Javelin 20 m/s not found","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N178": {"text":"Gao, Abeysekera, Hirvonen & Aschan (2003), The effect of footwear sole abrasion on the coefficient of friction on melting and hard ice, Int. J. Industrial Ergonomics 31(5) 323–330: kinetic friction on melting ice 0.055, on hard ice 0.18 (new soles) to 0.244 (abraded)","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N179": {"text":"Burnfield & Powers (2004), Prediction of slip events during walking: an analysis of utilized coefficient of friction and available slip resistance, AAFS C52: the peak utilized friction of walking 0.21 ± 0.04 (38 people, 96.6 m/min); a slip is likely when the friction used exceeds what the floor offers","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N180": {"text":"US National Weather Service glossary, blizzard (forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=bliz; weather.gov/dtx/winter_defs): sustained wind or frequent gusts to 35 mph or more, and falling or blowing snow reducing visibility frequently below 1/4 mile, for 3 hours or longer","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N181": {"text":"Pugh, L. G. C. E. (1971) The influence of wind resistance in running and walking and the mechanical efficiency of work against horizontal or vertical forces, J. Physiol. 213(2):255-276, doi:10.1113/jphysiol.1971.sp009381: O2 uptake rises with wind velocity squared; work against wind more efficient than against gravity. CdA 0.45 m2 not in the abstract (full text blocked)","opened":false, "kind": "science"},
 "N182": {"text":"Seliga, Hazen & Burnley, nowcasting snowfall rate from visibility (AMS paper 104640): S = c σ, c from about 0.6 early in an event to 0.2 late (0.16–0.31 in its cases); with Rasmussen, Vivekanandan, Cole, Myers & Masters (1999), J. Appl. Meteor.: the relation varies with the snow's type up to tenfold; the rate read as mm/h of water, to confirm","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N183": {"text":"Atlas, D. (1953) Optical extinction by rainfall, J. Meteorology 10:486-488, as given in Mulvey, G. J. (1973) The investigation of snowfall rate using optical techniques, M.S. thesis, SUNY Albany: V = 9.3 R^-0.67 km (theory, Marshall-Palmer drops), so sigma = 3.912/V = 0.42 R^0.67 per km; empirical (Bergeron rain) V = 11.6 R^-0.63, so sigma = 0.34 R^0.63","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N184": {"text":"Harper, B. A., Kepert, J. D. & Ginger, J. D. (2010) Guidelines for converting between various wind averaging periods in tropical cyclone conditions, WMO/TD-No. 1555 (factors from the 2008 AMS draft, paper 4B.1): 3-s gust / 10-min mean: off-land 1.52, in-land 1.66; 1.42-1.44 against a 2-3 min mean","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N185": {"text":"Design: Albert, Nov 5 18:07–18:08: pace by the floor, the weather, mass and gravity, never by the calendar (Pandolf); the effects law, boosts add once, cuts one at a time on what is left, never 0 or 100%; weather one system by an intensity from the dice, the blizzard by the NWS; grip by measured friction; crampons for foot and haulers, horses wait for horseshoes; weather visuals by code and math (SoRen's)","opened":true,"kind":"design"},
 "N186": {"text":"Rain classes per MANOBS via Barani Design; Beaufort scale (Wikipedia): Violent rain >50 mm/h (MANOBS, not shown as Met Office); Beaufort 8 gale 17.2-20.7 m/s. Snow 5 mm/h water not found","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
 "N187": {"text":"Locatelli, J. D. & Hobbs, P. V. (1974) Fall speeds and masses of solid precipitation particles, J. Geophys. Res. 79(15):2185-2197: Unrimed dendrite aggregates 1-14 mm fall 0.5-1.4 m/s","opened":true, "kind": "science"},
